WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.560
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Thomas Gabor, who's a criminologist and

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:10.251
author of five books on gun violence, including
American Carnage Shattering the Myths that

00:00:10.251 --> 00:00:12.099
Fuel Gun Violence.

00:00:12.099 --> 00:00:14.870
Tom, really appreciate your time today.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:15.870
Thanks for joining me.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.750
Speaker 5: My pleasure to be with you, David.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:23.330
Speaker 1: So, I mean, let's just start with
a very simple premise and see if there is

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:24.550
anything to it.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:30.740
Some of those in the United States who don't
want to see any additional gun safety regulations

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:37.630
or laws put in place say they wouldn't do
anything to reduce shootings anyway.

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:42.430
Is that a claim that we can actually debunk
with with data?

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:45.530
Do gun safety regulations reduce shootings?

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:48.070
Speaker 5: Certain ones do.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:54.200
And, you know, I just came across another
report today that showed, for example, that

00:00:54.200 --> 00:01:02.519
red states are states that are run mostly
by a governor, a governor who's a Republican

00:01:02.519 --> 00:01:09.830
or a Republican legislature, which tend to
have weaker gun laws, have much higher gun

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.890
mortality rates.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:17.619
So there's a lot of evidence that, first of
all, there's a correlation between gun ownership

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:19.189
and gun mortality.

00:01:19.189 --> 00:01:25.740
There's a tendency that gun ownership levels
go up in the states and there's huge variations

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.060
there.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:34.490
They there tends to be an increase in gun
mortality or gun deaths and gun homicides

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:35.630
as well.

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:42.140
So and as well, we see when we rate states
in terms of the weakness or strength of the

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:47.090
gun laws, the stronger the gun laws, the lower
gun mortality tends to be.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:53.649
Speaker 1: What can you give a couple of examples
of the types of regulations that are effective

00:01:53.649 --> 00:01:57.270
and then maybe a couple of examples of the
ones that maybe aren't so much?

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:58.780
Speaker 5: Okay.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:00.299
Well, good question.

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:06.950
First of all, I've studied gun licensing around
the world, and that requires a much thorough,

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:12.220
more thorough background check than we do
here in the United States, where you have

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:17.280
interviews with law enforcement, you have
reference checks and so forth.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.459
And those countries tend to do a whole lot
better than we do.

00:02:20.459 --> 00:02:26.060
And we see some states that have some kind
of owner licensing, such as Connecticut, that

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:31.970
have done better than other states, and they've
seen gun homicides go down.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:35.840
Another one is storage.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:42.030
Safe storage seems to make an enormous difference
or what we also call child access prevention

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.030
laws.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:52.700
So when we have consequences for people not
securely storing their guns in terms of legal

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.909
consequences, that seems to make a difference.

00:02:55.909 --> 00:03:04.670
Because, for example, we've seen many school
shooters, these young men who've either often

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:10.110
obtained their guns not always, but often
from the home or the home of a relative, and

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:12.140
these guns were not secured.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:18.010
Now, as far as what doesn't work, because,
you know, it's only in the last few years

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:21.790
that the federal government has been able
to conduct research because there's been an

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:28.160
attempt by allies of the gun lobby to stop
all research in this area.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.580
We hear a lot about universal background checks.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:37.000
I'd like to make a clarification there, because
what that means is that and this is why people

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:45.239
are seeking that every individual buys a gun,
has to have a background check.

00:03:45.239 --> 00:03:50.769
So today we have this you might call it a
loophole where if you buy on the private market,

00:03:50.769 --> 00:03:57.170
you buy through the Internet or from gun stores,
you don't need to go undergo a background

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:00.150
check if you've not bought from a licensed
dealer.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.680
So I'm all for that.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.549
However, the background check system also,
in my view, is broken.

00:04:05.549 --> 00:04:12.880
And if all we do is expand background checks
to all sales, it's not sufficient because

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:17.049
there are enormous problems with the system
itself.

00:04:17.049 --> 00:04:20.760
It's a two minute check of three FBI databases.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:26.040
And so we've had people like the Parkland
shooter who showed all kinds of disturbing

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:35.960
signs, but because he didn't have a criminal
felony record, he was able to buy his AR 15

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:37.750
legally from a gun store.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:43.900
So we need the analogy I use is, you know,
we pretty well have universal checks.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:47.630
When we get on a plane, everybody gets checked.

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:53.270
But the second question is not just does everybody
get checked, but how good is that detection?

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:54.270
Right.

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:56.780
That's through a lot of guns and explosives.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:05:00.440
Then you can check everybody, but it's not
sufficient.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:04.500
So what I'm saying here is the background,
the quality of the background check system

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:05.970
itself has to be improved.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:10.120
I don't believe that just expanding background
checks is sufficient.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:17.180
Speaker 1: What about ideas like restricting
the ability to purchase certain firearms to

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:19.220
individuals of a certain age?

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:24.820
So maybe if you're under 25, certain firearms
would not be legal, legally purchasable?

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:29.840
I mean, with all these things, you can always
say, well, then that person will figure out

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:31.789
how to get that firearm illegally.

00:05:31.789 --> 00:05:33.080
But it's like, maybe they won't.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:34.080
Some of them won't.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.210
Speaker 5: Right.

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:40.160
And you know, that argument that, you know,
criminals will always be criminals.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:41.690
And it's such a gross oversimplification.

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:42.690
Yeah.

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.870
You know, and therefore, we can't stop somebody
who.

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:47.479
Determined to get a gun?

00:05:47.479 --> 00:05:53.860
Well, first of all, a lot of gun violence
isn't committed, isn't premeditated, it's

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:54.910
spontaneous.

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:59.639
So if you happen to be carrying around the
gun and you get into a fight or a road rage

00:05:59.639 --> 00:06:02.380
incident, you're more likely to pull that
gun.

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:07.550
But the other thing is that if we use that
logic, we get rid of all laws because you

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:13.860
can't stop all molestation or rape or homicide
and so forth.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:16.650
So but some people are deterred.

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:21.690
And you make it more difficult because what's
easier than allowing somebody who is showing

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:28.750
some disturbing behavior to simply because
they don't have a criminal record on file,

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:34.240
especially a felony record, to just walk in
and purchase a gun in 2 minutes.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:35.240
Right.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:40.310
You know, and they could be also very aggravated,
angry about something.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:47.120
So I think, though, the age issue that you
mentioned would be helpful because we do have

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:51.820
quite a few people are committing gun violence.

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:59.870
After that, it becomes much more difficult
legally because a person can argue in court

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:04.960
that, well, I'm you know, I'm old enough to
go fight for the country.

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:06.650
I'm old enough to vote.

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:12.099
It also, you know, you don't have the right
to kind of a breach my rights.

00:07:12.099 --> 00:07:15.580
Speaker 1: And, Tom, we lost we lost your
file.

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:21.340
We lost your audio just for a second where
I think what you were saying was it's easier

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:25.470
to restrict based on based on age before people
are 18.

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:26.810
Was that the argument you were making?

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:29.250
But that these in-between ages can be tough
legally?

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:34.090
Speaker 5: Yeah, though, you know, 18 to 21,
I mean, that's passed in a number of states

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:41.160
in the past in Florida as well, before the
legislature tried to repeal that.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:42.160
Yeah.

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:45.919
So 18 to 21, I say, is probably as high as
you can go.

00:07:45.919 --> 00:07:46.919
Got it.

00:07:46.919 --> 00:07:51.789
And there are people challenging that as well,
saying that they can vote, they could serve

00:07:51.789 --> 00:07:54.270
the country at 18 and so forth.

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:56.229
So they should be able to purchase a gun.

00:07:56.229 --> 00:08:01.819
But leaving the legal arguments alone, I think
18 to 21, certainly those are some of the

00:08:01.819 --> 00:08:05.590
most active years for people committed to
gun violence.

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:08.180
So raising that age can be helpful.

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:12.849
Speaker 1: I want to talk about the issue
of mental health.

00:08:12.849 --> 00:08:15.129
And there are so many aspects to this.

00:08:15.129 --> 00:08:22.060
You know, on the one hand, colloquially speaking,
without diagnosing anyone, it when I think

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:27.690
about a mass shooting, it seems that we would
say anyone who gets to a point where this

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:34.640
is something they would go and do, they're
probably, quote, mentally ill in some way

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:37.680
because no sane person would do that.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:42.529
This is a very simplistic thing to say, but
in some sense, many of us would say no sane

00:08:42.529 --> 00:08:47.080
person who's emotionally regulated and whatever,
whatever commits such an act.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:51.790
So in a sense, certainly mental health is
a component.

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:54.120
But what do we really mean when we say that?

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:57.950
Are we talking about somebody who has anxiety
and sees a therapist?

00:08:57.950 --> 00:09:02.410
What is it exactly that we're trying to get
at when we talk about mental health?

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:07.070
How do you believe the mental health layer
should be applied to the gun safety regulations

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:08.070
layer?

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:10.670
Speaker 5: Well, we call it the mental illness.

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:11.670
Dodge.

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:16.230
There's a world of difference between saying
that somebody has a severe mental illness

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:23.310
like schizophrenia, bipolar disease, psychosis
or something like that.

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:29.560
And only 4% of people commit violent crimes
of the country have a severe mental illness.

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:34.940
Now, what you're talking about is often individuals
who are agitated.

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:43.120
In fact, one of the frightening statistics
in this country is that over 20 million individuals

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:46.030
have serious anger issues.

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:54.269
There are people who have a short trigger,
if you wish, and are quick to anger, have

00:09:54.269 --> 00:09:58.260
impulsive anger issues and also own guns.

00:09:58.260 --> 00:10:00.160
This is a much bigger problem.

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:06.540
So, yes, I mean, when you're talking about
somebody who commits a mass shooting or even

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:13.750
a shooting of one individual, that they're
agitated, they're angry, but we all get angry

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:15.870
at times.

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:21.240
And so there's a world of difference between
somebody suffering from major mental illness

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:27.990
and they're usually more of a danger to themselves
in terms of suicides, and they're more likely

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:29.450
to be victims of crime.

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:35.380
We see that with the homeless, some of the
homeless mentally ill who are victimized in

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:42.630
different cities, but they're much less of
a concern unless they also had, in addition

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:45.670
to a serious mental illness, a history of
violence.

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:53.790
I'd be more concerned with people who are
already at a moment's notice to deploy guns

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:58.180
against intruders or people who may be an
intruder.

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:04.399
You know, these recent incidents where somebody
took a turn around driveway and was shot at

00:11:04.399 --> 00:11:08.940
and somebody else who knocked on the wrong
door, I'm more concerned about all these people

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:14.880
now because they've been sold the gun lobby's
narrative that you're safer with a gun in

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:15.880
the home.

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:20.040
And, you know, the gun lobby has done a lot
to stoke people's fears and they're ready.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:26.279
They have 40% now of people have guns in the
home, have the guns loaded and accessible.

00:11:26.279 --> 00:11:27.800
That's what I'm talking about.

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:35.709
And if they also have a shorter trigger and
they're also afraid, very, very afraid, because

00:11:35.709 --> 00:11:41.709
this fear has been stoked for decades now,
I'm more concerned about those individuals

00:11:41.709 --> 00:11:46.339
because the guns are at the ready and they're
primed to commit an act of violence.

00:11:46.339 --> 00:11:53.139
Speaker 1: Yet you're getting to now going
beyond mental illness into a cultural component.

00:11:53.139 --> 00:11:54.139
And one of the.

00:11:54.139 --> 00:11:57.930
Things that is different in the United States
than in many other countries where I've spent

00:11:57.930 --> 00:12:04.570
time is that there is a gun culture, which
I describe it as.

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:12.980
It sees firearms as a more valid way to resolve
interpersonal issues, frustrations, political

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:16.269
issues, etc., which access a side.

00:12:16.269 --> 00:12:17.721
And of course, we have to talk about access.

00:12:17.721 --> 00:12:23.300
Access aside, I don't see in a lot of the
other countries where I've spent time is this

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:26.710
intangible cultural difference a reality in
the United States?

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:29.199
Speaker 5: Well, you know, this is what we
take on.

00:12:29.199 --> 00:12:36.970
One of the myths we take on in our book American
Carnage, because in reality, first of all,

00:12:36.970 --> 00:12:40.750
there is no all encompassing gun culture in
the United States.

00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:47.220
We have entire regions like New England, where
you have gun ownership levels in certain areas,

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:48.630
only 15 to 20%.

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:49.630
Right.

00:12:49.630 --> 00:12:53.940
Only a third of American adults are gun owners.

00:12:53.940 --> 00:13:02.350
Now, if you're talking about a minority of
individuals who you might say have a gun fetish

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:07.740
in stock or a large arsenal of guns, we're
talking about maybe what we call the super

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:15.170
owners who are 6% of the American population
and they own two thirds of all the guns.

00:13:15.170 --> 00:13:16.170
Wow.

00:13:16.170 --> 00:13:18.870
That doesn't make them all dangerous at all.

00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:25.330
But these are the people who have more of
a gun fetish, if you want to call it that.

00:13:25.330 --> 00:13:28.260
And some of them are just collectors.

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:31.050
But the vast majority of the population isn't
like that.

00:13:31.050 --> 00:13:36.790
But let me tell you, what's changed is in
1977, the National Rifle Association got taken

00:13:36.790 --> 00:13:44.949
over by a very extremist group of people,
and they have now spent over 40 years trying

00:13:44.949 --> 00:13:53.570
to convince Americans that if you arm yourself
in the home and carry guns, you're going to

00:13:53.570 --> 00:13:54.980
be a lot more safe.

00:13:54.980 --> 00:14:00.290
The other thing they did is because with the
decline of hunting and the end of the draft

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:05.220
and the military draft was where many people
were introduced to guns and the increasing

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:10.670
urbanization of the country, there was a real
crunch on the gun industry.

00:14:10.670 --> 00:14:14.339
So they started pushing this narrative of
self-protection.

00:14:14.339 --> 00:14:19.769
So this is only about 40 years old, this narrative
that we should all have guns.

00:14:19.769 --> 00:14:22.509
We're safer with guns in the home carrying
guns.

00:14:22.509 --> 00:14:31.000
In fact, in the 1800s, most of the states
banned the carrying of concealed firearms.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:35.420
So we actually in the United States have a
history going back to the revolutionary period

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:41.779
of regulating firearms, even having citizens
forming a militia and asking them once a year

00:14:41.779 --> 00:14:45.370
to show up at the town square to show why
the guns you have.

00:14:45.370 --> 00:14:51.029
And those guns could be seized by the militia
if there was an attack on the country or some

00:14:51.029 --> 00:14:53.699
revolution to put down.

00:14:53.699 --> 00:14:59.079
So we've had a history, actually, the Old
West, you had gun ordinances, but it's more

00:14:59.079 --> 00:15:03.880
recently the last 40 years that we've departed
from that history.

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:11.540
And what you call a gun culture is really
an attempt by extremists and a gun lobby to

00:15:11.540 --> 00:15:16.050
promote the idea of the armed citizenry being
safer.

00:15:16.050 --> 00:15:20.780
But we see that it's not the you know, it's
so far from the truth.

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:26.160
And what we're seeing is the opposite more
mass shooting, more gun deaths, children now,

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:32.810
the number one cause of death of Americans
up to 19 is is gunfire and so forth.

00:15:32.810 --> 00:15:36.800
So things have really moved in the in the
opposite direction from what they're claiming.

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:42.639
Speaker 1: The book is American Carnage Shattering
the Myths That Fuel Gun Violence.

00:15:42.639 --> 00:15:46.310
We've been speaking with Thomas Gabor, criminologist.

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:48.000
Really appreciate your time and insights today.

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:49.000
Speaker 5: Thank you, David.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:49.004
It's been a pleasure.

